1. Ingestion (CCaaS Lambda / landing_lambda.py)

What it does: Acts as the "quality gate" between raw incoming files and the data lake.

Checks every incoming survey/contact file for corruption — specifically catches a known issue where Excel mangles long ID numbers into scientific notation (e.g., an ID turning into 5.16636E+19). Bad files are automatically quarantined and the team gets an alert instead of bad data flowing downstream.
Extracts a reliable "event date" from each file, with several fallback options in case the primary date field is missing.
Sorts clean files into the right folder structure by date so downstream systems can find them.
** Date format standardization: Before writing files downstream, the Lambda checks the date fields in each record and converts them into one consistent date format. This prevents downstream systems (Glue jobs, dashboards) from seeing the same date represented in multiple different formats depending on which survey or source it came from.

2. Survey Curation (datalake-ccaas-sdap-survey-curation)

What it does: Takes the raw survey exports and turns them into clean, standardized data by survey type.

Identifies which survey each record belongs to (IVR phone survey, Web pop-up survey, Web relational survey, Email, Chat, etc.) by reading naming prefixes on the incoming data — made this matching resistant to inconsistent capitalization from the vendor, which had been silently causing whole surveys to disappear from reports.
Always picks up the most recently updated file for a given day, rather than accidentally picking an older file.
Produces a daily summary report (record counts by survey and by day) that feeds Power BI/Tableau dashboards, so the business can track survey volumes without needing to query raw data.
B
**atch-level deduplication

Beyond just standardizing survey data, this job also removes duplicate records within each processing batch — so if the same survey response was accidentally sent more than once by the vendor, it's only counted once in the curated data.
The number of duplicates removed is tracked and reported after every run, so the team can see how much duplication is happening upstream over time.

**Data quality checks

New/unexpected field detection: Every run compares the fields coming in from the vendor against the expected list of survey questions. If a field shows up that isn't recognized, it's flagged (not silently dropped) and surfaced in a summary alert — this is what caught the vendor's inconsistent capitalization issue and a missing survey type before it caused ongoing data loss.
Survey summary report: After each run, a report is generated and emailed showing record counts per survey type per day, plus any flagged/unexpected fields — giving the team an early warning system instead of finding gaps later in a dashboard.
Latest-file validation: The job double-checks it picked up the most recently updated file for the day (not just an older file that happened to sort first alphabetically), so reports don't run on stale data.

3. Sentiment Analysis — Raw Processing (datalake-sentiment-analysis-dev)

What it does: Prepares survey responses for AI-based sentiment scoring.

Removes duplicate survey responses (customers sometimes submit the same survey more than once, or the same interaction gets logged twice).
For phone (IVR) surveys, keeps only responses tied to the "Main" campaign, filtering out test/other campaign noise, and logs exactly how many records were excluded for transparency.
Tracks, per day and per survey source, whether new data actually arrived — avoiding wasted reprocessing when nothing changed.

Schema consistency checks: When reading historical data, the job reconciles differences in field types across days (e.g., a field stored as text on one day and as a number on another) so reports don't break or show gaps due to a technical mismatch.
Duplicate validation reporting: For phone (IVR), pop-up (TXN), and relational (REL) surveys, the dedupe logic produces a validation report showing exactly how many records were removed and why (exact repeat, missing comment, or same comment on a different date) — so the team can audit what's being filtered rather than trusting it blindly.
New-data tracking: Before processing, the job compares record counts against what's already stored for that day, so it can confirm whether genuinely new data arrived versus reprocessing the same batch.


4. Sentiment Analysis — Curation/Enrichment (datalake-sentiment-analysis-curation)

What it does: Adds customer context to the sentiment-scored survey data.

Joins in customer account information (arrears status, account details, meter/service restoration status) so sentiment scores can be sliced by customer segment.
Always pulls this account reference data from the trusted production source, regardless of which environment the job runs in, so account details are never out of date.
Always uses the latest available snapshot of that reference data.
Writes the final, enriched result to a dedicated location that Tableau/Power BI/Athena read from for reporting.

Join-key validation: Before matching survey records to customer account data, the job actively checks that the account number field has a real, non-blank value (not just that the column technically exists) — preventing bad or empty matches from silently passing through.
Latest-snapshot selection: When multiple versions of the customer reference data exist, the job always identifies and uses the most current one, preventing enrichment from running against outdated account information.
Trusted-source enforcement: Customer account data is always pulled from the verified production source regardless of which environment the job runs in — so testing/development activity can never accidentally introduce inconsistent account data into reporting.